* text=auto eol=lf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# WHAT GITHUB REPORTS THIS REPOSITORY TO BE — AND THE OVER-CORRECTION THAT CAME FIRST.
#
# Measured at v2.0.0: `gh api repos/.../languages` returned **Python 116,487 bytes and nothing
# else** — 100% Python for a repository whose substance is 35 language packs, a contract and a set
# of worked examples. Linguist treats Markdown as prose and excludes it, so the one directory
# holding code took the whole bar.
#
# The fix applied then was a BLANKET `*.md linguist-detectable=true`, and it over-corrected in the
# other direction: 464 KB of guides, operating cards and design notes entered the bar and GitHub
# reported **61% Markdown**, describing a routing table with a contract as a documentation
# repository. Both readings were wrong, and both were wrong the same way — a blanket rule applied
# to files that are not all the same kind of thing.
#
# THE ACCURATE CLASSIFICATION IS PER KIND, not per suffix:
#   - guides, cards, patterns, research and wiki pages ARE documentation, and Linguist's default
#     already says so. The override was the inaccuracy; removing it is not hiding anything.
#   - CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md and llms.txt are GENERATED by `atlas.py index --write`. Marking them
#     generated is literally true, and it also collapses them in every diff, where hand-reading a
#     regenerated file is wasted review.
#   - the examples ARE code, in the language they claim, and stay detectable.
#   - the manifests and atlas.yaml ARE the declaration this repository runs on, so they stay
#     detectable while workflow YAML (configuration) does not.
#
# `atlas.py check` asserts that every file in atlas.yaml/generated_files is marked generated here,
# so the two rosters cannot drift.
#
# PROJECTED at contract v2.18.0 by scripts/langbar.py, which computes it from the tree rather than
# predicting it: Python 70.1%, YAML 25.4%, then Go, Shell, TypeScript, Rust, C++, SQL and C.
# That is what the CODE in this repository is. The prose is still 45% of the bytes and is still
# there to read; it is simply not a programming language.
examples/** linguist-detectable=true
examples/** linguist-documentation=false
examples/** linguist-vendored=false
languages/**/tools.yaml linguist-detectable=true
atlas.yaml linguist-detectable=true

# GENERATED, AND SAYING SO IS A FACT RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE. `atlas.py index --write` writes
# these whole; `check` fails on any drift from what the declaration renders.
CLAUDE.md linguist-generated=true
AGENTS.md linguist-generated=true
CHAT.md linguist-generated=true
llms.txt linguist-generated=true
.agent/bootstrap.json linguist-generated=true

# The banner is content-addressed and binary; it is not a source file.
docs/assets/** linguist-vendored=true

# SWIFT IS EXCLUDED FROM DETECTION, AND THE REASON IS NOT COSMETIC.
# Making the examples detectable (above) expanded CodeQL default setup to every language it could
# see, including Swift — and `Analyze (swift)` FAILS, because CodeQL must BUILD Swift and autobuild
# cannot build a single file that requires `swiftc -parse-as-library`. There is no Swift project
# here: there is one example, whose own assertions `exrun.py` runs on any machine with swiftc.
# A scanner that fails on every run gets ignored, and an ignored scanner covers nothing.
# TRIGGER TO REVERSE THIS: a Package.swift beside the example, which autobuild can build. Then
# delete these two lines and let CodeQL analyse it.
examples/swift/** linguist-detectable=false
*.swift linguist-detectable=false
